\section{Theoretical Analysis of \name{}}
\label{app:dfb-theory}
\label{app:theory}

This appendix proves the \cref{lem:rare-bottleneck} and the \cref{prop:density-bellman}, then compares the local error weighting of the two objectives. 
The reconstruction analysis concerns exact SMs at fixed rank; the Bellman analysis concerns a fixed policy. 

\subsection{Proof of the rare-bottleneck lemma}
\label{app:bottleneck-proof}

Fix the policy, dynamics, reward, and reference distribution.
On a finite state space $\{x_j\}_{j=1}^{m}$ with $\rho_j>0$, write each SM as a row vector of state masses, $M_i=(M^\pi(s_i,a_i,\{x_j\}))_{j=1}^{m}$.
Squared density-ratio regression uses
\begin{equation}
    \|v\|_\rho^2 := \sum_{j=1}^{m}\frac{v_j^2}{\rho_j}, \qquad G_r=\langle d,r\rangle=\sum_{j=1}^{m}d_jr(x_j), \label{eq:app-bottleneck-norm}
\end{equation}
where $d=M_1-M_2$ is the difference between the two bottleneck rows. The inverse reference weights arise because these vectors contain measure masses, not density ratios.

Let the bottleneck rows have positive weights $w_1,w_2$ with $w_1+w_2=w$. 
Let the remaining rows have weights $(1-w)\alpha_i$, where $\alpha_i>0$ and $\sum_{i\in\mathcal O}\alpha_i=1$.
The rank-$k$ approximation minimizes
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal L_w(\widehat M) =(1-w)\sum_{i\in\mathcal O} \alpha_i\|M_i-\widehat M_i\|_\rho^2 +\sum_{\ell=1}^{2} w_\ell\|M_\ell-\widehat M_\ell\|_\rho^2, \qquad \operatorname{rank}(\widehat M)\leq k. \label{eq:app-bottleneck-objective}
\end{equation}
Replacing both bottleneck rows by their arithmetic mean $\overline M=(M_1+M_2)/2$ incurs pairwise error
\begin{equation}
    \sum_{\ell=1}^{2} w_\ell\|M_\ell-\overline M\|_\rho^2 =\frac{w}{4}\|d\|_\rho^2. \label{eq:app-mean-collapse}
\end{equation}
This error vanishes as $w\to0$, whereas $G_r$ is unchanged.
This is the error on these two rows, not the excess loss of a rank-$k$ fit constrained to preserve the gap.

To characterize the optimal reconstruction, let $R=\operatorname{diag}(\rho_1,\ldots,\rho_m)$ and $Y_i=M_iR^{-1/2}$.
Given an Euclidean column norm, the relevant Gram matrices are
\begin{equation}
    C_{\mathrm{ord}} =\sum_{i\in\mathcal O}\alpha_iY_i^\top Y_i, \qquad C_w =(1-w)C_{\mathrm{ord}} +\sum_{\ell=1}^{2}w_\ell Y_\ell^\top Y_\ell. \label{eq:app-bottleneck-gram}
\end{equation}
For a fixed subspace, projection minimizes each row's squared error. 
The best $k$-dimensional subspace is spanned by the leading eigenvectors of $C_w$. 
Let $U_w$ contain these orthonormal eigenvectors and set $\Pi_k^w=R^{-1/2}U_wU_w^\top R^{1/2}$.
The optimal rows are $\widehat M_i=M_i\Pi_k^w$.

Since $C_w\to C_{\mathrm{ord}}$, the assumed eigengap $\lambda_k(C_{\mathrm{ord}}) >\lambda_{k+1}(C_{\mathrm{ord}})$ implies convergence of the leading-subspace projectors, and hence $\Pi_k^w\to\Pi_k^0$. Consequently,
\begin{equation}
    G_{r,k}^w =\langle d\Pi_k^w,r\rangle \longrightarrow \langle d\Pi_k^0,r\rangle. \label{eq:app-projected-gap}
\end{equation}
In particular, $d\Pi_k^0=0$ implies $G_{r,k}^w\to0$ despite the unchanged true gap. Thus rarity suppresses the contribution of the bottleneck rows to subspace selection; it does not force every rare action gap to disappear.
\hfill$\square$




\subsection{Proof of the Bellman fixed-point proposition}
\label{app:bellman-operator}
\label{app:population-objective}

Fix $z$, its policy $\pi_z$, and $0\leq\gamma<1$, and write the model distribution as $\nu_\theta=h_\theta\rho$.
For a fixed target distribution $\bar\nu$, define
\begin{equation}
    (\mathcal T_z\bar\nu)(s,a,\cdot) =(1-\gamma)P(\cdot\mid s,a) +\gamma \E_{\substack{ s'\sim P(\cdot\mid s,a)\\ a'\sim\pi_z(\cdot\mid s') }} [\bar\nu(s',a',\cdot)]. \label{eq:app-dfb-operator}
\end{equation}
This is a probability distribution because its two components have total weights $1-\gamma$ and $\gamma$.

Assume that every target used in the recursion has a density $t=d(\mathcal T_z\bar\nu)/d\rho$, is exactly representable, and is fitted at every relevant state--action pair.
Assume also that the losses below are finite.
At a fixed $(s,a)$, the loss is $\ell(h;\bar\nu)=-\E_\rho[t\log h]$, and
\begin{equation}
    \ell(h_\theta;\bar\nu)-\ell(t;\bar\nu) =\E_\rho\!\left[t\log\frac{t}{h_\theta}\right] =\operatorname{KL}(t\rho\,\|\,h_\theta\rho). \label{eq:app-ce-bellman}
\end{equation}
The loss is therefore minimized by $h_\theta=t$: exact cross-entropy fitting performs one Bellman update.

For distribution-valued functions, define $D(\nu_1,\nu_2) =\sup_{s,a} \|\nu_1(s,a,\cdot)-\nu_2(s,a,\cdot)\|_{\mathrm{TV}}$.
The one-step terms cancel, and averaging cannot increase total variation, so
\begin{equation}
    D(\mathcal T_z\nu_1,\mathcal T_z\nu_2) \leq\gamma D(\nu_1,\nu_2). \label{eq:app-contraction}
\end{equation}
The normalized SM $\nu^{\pi_z}=(1-\gamma)M^{\pi_z}$ satisfies this Bellman equation.
The contraction makes it the unique fixed point and implies convergence of repeated exact updates.
\hfill$\square$

The minibatch softmax replaces $\rho$ by an empirical candidate distribution; its update need not equal the population update above. The proposition does not establish convergence with restricted scores, uncovered state--action pairs, or a changing actor.



\subsection{What normalization and the categorical objective change}
\label{app:loss-geometry}

Multiplying the SM target by $1-\gamma$ only rescales the optimal linear reconstruction and leaves its selected subspace unchanged. 
With the softmax, the bilinear factorization parameterizes scores rather than successor densities, so the predicted probability matrix need not have rank $k$.
The loss also changes how relative errors are weighted.
On a finite support, take $\nu_j>0$ and $\rho_j>0$, and let $\widehat\nu_j=\nu_j(1+\epsilon_j)$ with small $\epsilon_j$ and $\sum_j\nu_j\epsilon_j=0$.
Squared density-ratio error gives
\begin{equation}
    \frac12\sum_j\rho_j \left(\frac{\widehat\nu_j-\nu_j}{\rho_j}\right)^2 =\frac12\sum_j\frac{\nu_j^2}{\rho_j}\epsilon_j^2. \label{eq:app-squared-local-weighting}
\end{equation}
For cross-entropy, the excess loss is
\begin{equation}
    \operatorname{KL}(\nu\,\|\,\widehat\nu) =-\sum_j\nu_j\log(1+\epsilon_j) =\frac12\sum_j\nu_j\epsilon_j^2 +o(\|\epsilon\|^2), \label{eq:app-ce-local-weighting}
\end{equation}
because normalization cancels the first-order term.
Thus the local weights are $\nu_j^2/\rho_j$ and $\nu_j$, respectively. Standard FB's unnormalized target adds only the common factor $(1-\gamma)^{-2}$ to the squared-error expression.

This comparison concerns future-state errors within one successor distribution. 
Both objectives still weight conditioning state--action pairs by their sampling frequency.
It therefore neither eliminates the rare-pair effect in the lemma nor guarantees that categorical fitting preserves every bottleneck gap.




\section{Reward Evaluation and Task Inference}
\label{app:fb-relation}

\subsection{Evaluating a reward under a represented policy}
\label{app:reward-readout}
\label{app:fb-task-map}

For a bounded state reward $r$, \name{} uses
\begin{equation}
    q_{\theta,r}(s,a,z) =\E_{s^+\sim\rho} [h_\theta(s,a,z,s^+)r(s^+)] =\frac{ \E_\rho[e^{f_\theta(s,a,z,s^+)}r(s^+)] }{ \E_\rho[e^{f_\theta(s,a,z,s^+)}] }. \label{eq:app-model-reward-readout}
\end{equation}
If the successor distribution is exact, then
\begin{equation}
    q_{\theta,r}(s,a,z) =\int r\,d\nu^{\pi_z}(s,a,\cdot) =(1-\gamma)Q_r^{\pi_z}(s,a), \label{eq:app-q-relation}
\end{equation}
which preserves action rankings.
The standard FB shortcut $F^\top\E_\rho[rB]$ no longer applies because $F^\top B/\tau$ is inside the normalized exponential.
Evaluating $r$ under a fixed $\pi_z$ and selecting an appropriate policy index $z$ are separate operations.

\subsection{Inferring a task index from reward samples}
\label{app:task-index}

Given states $u_j\sim\rho$ and rewards $r_j=r(u_j)$, let $b_j=B_\theta(u_j)$ and $\bar r=M^{-1}\sum_jr_j$.
Let $\hat\mu$ and $\hat C$ be the running estimates of the mean and covariance of $B_\theta$ maintained during pretraining.
Here, \emph{centering} means subtracting a mean: we use $b_j-\hat\mu$ and $r_j-\bar r$.

Set $v=\operatorname{tr}(\hat C)/d$ and $\lambda=0.01v$.
Let $\widetilde C$ denote $\hat C$ after replacing eigenvalues below $10^{-6}v$ by that threshold.
We solve the regularized linear system
\begin{equation}
    (\widetilde C+\lambda I)c_r =\frac1M\sum_{j=1}^{M} (b_j-\hat\mu)(r_j-\bar r), \qquad z_r=\sqrt d\,\frac{c_r}{\|c_r\|}. \label{eq:app-task-index}
\end{equation}
This estimates a regularized linear reward approximation $\widetilde r(s) =\bar r+(B_\theta(s)-\hat\mu)^\top c_r$ using running feature moments and finite reward samples; it is not an exact least-squares coefficient.
Centering makes this construction unchanged by a constant shift of the backward features.

When $\|c_r\|<10^{-6}$, the implementation returns a fixed task vector of norm $\sqrt d$ instead of normalizing $c_r$.
A small coefficient does not establish that $r$ is constant: the reward may have little detected linear association with the learned features.

The map accepts any state reward evaluated on the sampled states, not only goal-reaching rewards.
During pretraining it supplies indices for sampled goal-reaching rewards; random task vectors are sampled directly. At evaluation, the same map converts downstream reward samples into a task index.

When the normalized index in Equation~\eqref{eq:app-task-index} is used, the pretraining reward $B_\theta(s)^\top z_r$ is a positive affine transformation of $\widetilde r(s)$.
This preserves optimal action rankings in the continuing discounted setting, but does not guarantee that the learned actor is optimal for the original reward $r$.
Reward approximation and policy learning remain separate sources of error.

The direct-goal comparison uses the goal supplied by the evaluation environment to specify policy conditioning, rather than identifying the task from reward-labelled states.
It is a diagnostic comparison between task-specification procedures, not a formal upper bound on reward-inference performance.